From PSchwarz at gcrinsight.com  Tue Jan  3 16:49:22 2006
From: PSchwarz at gcrinsight.com (Schwarz,Paul)
Date: Tue, 03 Jan 2006 08:49:22 -0800
Subject: Summary: how to extract entire rows from a data frame based on a index variable?
Message-ID: <89C7DEAADB79564DB278694EB2417CCE77394F@CHOCOLATE.GCRInsight.com>

A while ago (unfortunately), I posted the following request for help on
extracting rows from data frames. I received several responses, some of
which involves sorting or ordering the data frame by the index variable
and/or using the unique() or !duplicated() functions. I appreciate all
the responses that I received. Thank you. The solution that I ended up
adopting was the following:

> index<-split(1:nrow(mydata),mydata$ID)
>
first<-sapply(index,function(x,y=mydata){x[min(y[x,"date"])==y[x,"date"]
][1]})
>
last<-sapply(index,function(x,y=mydata){x[max(y[x,"date"])==y[x,"date"]]
[1]})
> mydata[first,]
> mydata[last,]

> ###Test
> mydata<-data.frame(ID=c(1,1,2,2,3,3,2,2,1,1),date=c(1,2,3,4,3,
> 4,3,2,5,6),oth
> er=c(1,2,3,4,5,10,12,13,14,15)
> 
> 
> Then mydata[first,] gives all the first records ordered by mydata$ID
> and the same for mydata[last,]
> 

Thanks again, everybody.

-Paul
-----Original Message-----
To: 's-news at lists.biostat.wustl.edu'
Subject: how to extract entire rows from a data frame based on a index
variable?

Dear S-News readers,

I'm puzzling over how to solve the following problem. Let's say that I
have a data frame with possibly multiple observations (rows) per
sampling unit, in this case survey respondents. In addition to a
respondent ID variable, there is a date variable tracking the date the
information was collected. If I want to extract from this data frame
only the most recent observation (or the first, etc.) for each
respondent, how could I do this? I've been thinking about this problem
as a tapply()-like problem where the respondent ID would be the index
variable, but I haven't figured how to write a function to extract and
return entire rows. I would appreciate ideas or suggestions from fellow
list readers on how to solve this.

Thanks for your help.

-Paul

