From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Tue Jan  3 21:32:05 2006
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Tue, 03 Jan 2006 21:32:05 +0000
Subject: Re: Assessing the residual deviance in GLM models with dichotomous
In-Reply-To: <89C7DEAADB79564DB278694EB2417CCE773998@CHOCOLATE.GCRInsight.com>
References: <89C7DEAADB79564DB278694EB2417CCE773998@CHOCOLATE.GCRInsight.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.61.0601032121280.7397@gannet.stats>

What is puzzling you is the following in stat.anova called 
by anova.glmlist:

     dev[nnas] <- 1 - pchisq(abs(dev[nnas]), abs(df[nnas]))

which takes the absolute value of the change in deviance, whether 
justified or not.  I think it is intended to allow one to be careless 
which is the larger model, but in that case the sign-flip should be the 
same on both terms.

You have an increase in deviance from a model that has more parameters.
That cannot happen if they are nested, but are they?  In particular, are 
the same missing values being omitted in the two models?

If q05b.01 ranges for 1 to 7, you have just added an indicator for
q05b.01 == 1 (not what I would call piecewise linear), so I wonder if that 
is what you intended?

